Cystic Fibrosis (Pancreatic Insufficiency)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Steatorrhea, Failure to thrive, Elevated sweat chloride

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-014
Reason for Visit: Worsening severe abdominal cramps, gross malabsorption, and significant dehydration in a patient with chronic respiratory and gastrointestinal symptoms, requiring urgent after-hours evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (adolescent) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a week-long exacerbation of profuse, greasy, foul-smelling diarrhea leading to marked lethargy, severe abdominal pain, and an inability to maintain hydration. This presentation demanded an urgent home visit to evaluate for severe intravascular volume depletion, intestinal obstruction (DIOS), and gross malnutrition prior to the next standard in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Severe stomach cramps, huge foul-smelling bowel movements, and intense fatigue. Patient states, My stomach hurts constantly, and no matter how much I drink, I still feel thirsty and completely exhausted.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a chronic history of the adolescent experiencing failure to thrive and poor weight gain despite a voracious appetite. Recently, symptoms have acutely worsened, characterized by frequent, foul-smelling, bulky, greasy, and pale stools (steatorrhea). Over the past 48 hours, the patient has developed significant abdominal distension and frequent crampy pain. There is a chronic, productive cough with thick purulent sputum, and a history of recurrent sinopulmonary infections. Parents recall the child having distinctly salty-tasting skin during infancy and a distant history of meconium ileus.
Functional Status: Weak and lethargic, resting on the couch. Functionally limited by abdominal pain and fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Significant risk of malabsorption-induced hypovolemia and vitamin deficiencies; parents are highly engaged and providing continuous care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 42.0 Kg (Underweight for age)
General: Appears markedly thin, pale, and fatigued, with visible generalized muscle wasting.
Physical Exam: Respiratory: Auscultation reveals bilateral coarse crackles and scattered wheezes, consistent with chronic airway disease. Integumentary/Extremities: Digital clubbing is visibly present on all fingers. Skin turgor is sluggish, and mucous membranes are dry, indicating moderate dehydration. Gastrointestinal: The diagnostic triad is clinically profound: the history confirms failure to thrive and current profuse steatorrhea. (Elevated sweat chloride is the expected confirmatory diagnostic test). The abdomen is markedly distended and tympanic, with diffuse, severe crampy tenderness but no focal rigidity. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Signs of chronic fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (A, D, E, K) are suspected given the gross malnutrition.
Environmental Observations: High-calorie nutritional supplements and pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT) bottles are visible on the counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has been non-compliant with prescribed pancreatic enzyme supplements during school hours.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cystic Fibrosis (Pancreatic Insufficiency) - Status: Acute Exacerbation/Decompensated
Differentials:

Celiac Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of prominent chronic suppurative respiratory symptoms (purulent cough, clubbing) and the classic history of meconium ileus, which define cystic fibrosis.

Distal Intestinal Obstruction Syndrome (DIOS) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profuse, massive diarrheal output rather than absolute constipation and bilious vomiting, though the distension requires close monitoring.

Acute Infectious Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The strictly greasy, bulky nature of the stool (steatorrhea) rather than watery or bloody diarrhea, coupled with the profound chronic failure to thrive history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe acute-on-chronic malabsorption secondary to pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, leading to profound steatorrhea and evolving intravascular volume depletion. The combination of bulky greasy stools, chronic purulent pulmonary disease, and failure to thrive represents the classic phenotype of Cystic Fibrosis. The acute exacerbation of gastrointestinal fluid loss and resulting hypotension (BP 95/60) required urgent after-hours evaluation to prevent hypovolemic shock. Delaying care until routine hours was unsafe given the patient's severe lethargy and abdominal pain. Immediate assessment allowed for the initiation of aggressive oral rehydration, optimization of pancreatic enzyme compliance, and stabilization of the patient pending an urgent multidisciplinary specialist review. The expected investigation results include an elevated sweat chloride concentration greater than 60 mmol/L on pilocarpine iontophoresis testing that would assist in confirming the diagnosis formally.

